//? Title: Inspect a partition with silhouette widths
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en

> Find an observation closer to a neighboring cluster

For each observation, let \(a\) be its mean dissimilarity to the other
members of its assigned cluster. Let \(b\) be its smallest mean
dissimilarity to another cluster. The silhouette width is
\((b-a)/\max(a,b)\). A singleton has width zero by convention.

Six values on a line come with three supplied labels. One value is
closer on average to a neighboring cluster than to the other members
of its assigned cluster. The example keeps the original labels while
computing observation widths and cluster means.

```{cpp, id=silhouette-partition, file="figures/silhouette_widths.svg", results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <datamunge/plot/plot.hpp>
#include <datamunge/stats/silhouette.hpp>

#include <array>
#include <cmath>
#include <filesystem>
#include <format>
#include <print>
#include <string>
#include <vector>

int main() {
    namespace dm = datamunge;
    const std::array x{0., 1., 5., 7., 8., 20.};
    dm::linalg::DenseMatrix<double> distance(x.size(), x.size());
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < x.size(); ++i)
        for (std::size_t j = 0; j < x.size(); ++j)
            distance(i, j) = std::abs(x[i] - x[j]);
    const auto fit = dm::stats::silhouette_analysis(distance, {10, 10, 10, 20, 20, 99});
    std::println("The overall mean silhouette width is {:.3f}.\n", fit.mean_width);
    std::println("| Cluster | Observations | Mean silhouette width |");
    std::println("| ---: | ---: | ---: |");
    for (const auto& c : fit.clusters)
        std::println("| {} | {} | {:.3f} |", c.label, c.count, c.mean_width);
    std::println("\nThe value 5 belongs to cluster {} and has nearest alternative cluster {}.",
        fit.observations[2].cluster, fit.observations[2].neighbor);

    auto plot = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
    plot.line({0, 0}, {.4, 6.6}, "", {113, 120, 132}, 1.2);
    std::vector<double> ticks;
    std::vector<std::string> names;
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < x.size(); ++i) {
        const auto& row = fit.observations[i];
        const double position = x.size() - i;
        ticks.push_back(position);
        names.push_back(std::format("{} (cluster {})", x[i], row.cluster));
        if (row.width == 0)
            plot.points({0}, {position}, "", {91, 97, 107}, 4);
        else
            plot.horizontal_bars({position}, {row.width}, "",
                row.width > 0 ? dm::graphics::RGBA{32, 96, 151} : dm::graphics::RGBA{178, 77, 46}, .62);
    }
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    plot.title("Silhouette widths for the supplied partition")
        .x_label("Silhouette width").y_label("").y_ticks(ticks, names)
        .x_limits(-1, 1).y_limits(.35, 6.65)
        .size(740, 420).save_svg("build/silhouette_widths.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
The overall mean silhouette width is 0.401.

| Cluster | Observations | Mean silhouette width |
| ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 10 | 3 | 0.257 |
| 20 | 2 | 0.817 |
| 99 | 1 | 0.000 |

The value 5 belongs to cluster 10 and has nearest alternative cluster 20.
\image(figures/silhouette_widths.svg)
// result_end
\caption(The value 5 has a negative width and the singleton at 20 has width zero.)
\alttext(Values 0 and 1 have positive silhouette widths near 0.6. Value 5 has width minus 0.44. Values 7 and 8 have widths above 0.8. The singleton at 20 is marked at zero.)

The negative width identifies the value 5 for closer inspection. Its
mean distance to its assigned cluster is 4.5, and its mean distance
to cluster 20 is 2.5. Cluster 20 has the largest mean width among
the three assigned clusters.

[Rousseeuw (1987)|https://doi.org/10.1016/0377-0427(87)90125-7]
